# config/subsys/Kconfig.entropy — Entropy sources and the TPM

choice
    prompt "CPU entropy instruction as a pool seed source"
    default ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_CREDIT
    help
      The CPU's own entropy instruction seeds the kernel entropy pool
      (kernel/src/boot/entropy.rs `install_entropy`, after virtio-rng and
      independent of it): riscv64 Zkr `seed` CSR (RV_ZKR), aarch64 FEAT_RNG
      RNDRRS (A64_RNG), x86_64 RDSEED, else RDRAND (X86_RDSEED,
      X86_RDRAND). Each instruction is used only where its own extension
      choice allows it and the boot probe finds it; without one the boot
      is exactly as before. The pool seeds the TCP ISN secret, the
      DHCP/DNS/NTP ids, the link keys, ring 3's `entropy` endpoint and
      the stack canary; without any credited source they stay on their
      runtime-counter paths.

      credit: mixed and credited, so the CPU alone seeds the pool on a
      board with no entropy device (Linux RANDOM_TRUST_CPU=y). mix-only:
      mixed, never credited; the pool still needs virtio-rng or the
      persisted seed (Linux RANDOM_TRUST_CPU=n). off: never read.

      Cost: one draw of 48 bytes at boot (RDSEED/RNDRRS: a few hundred
      cycles per 8 bytes; Zkr: 16 bits per CSR read); nothing after.

    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_CREDIT
        bool "credit: mix and credit (trust the CPU)"
    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_MIX
        bool "mix-only: mix, never credit"
    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_OFF
        bool "off: never read the CPU's entropy instruction"
endchoice

config ENTROPY_CPU_RETRIES
    int "CPU entropy instruction: retries per word"
    range 1 100000
    default 1000
    help
      How many times the boot retries one word of the CPU entropy
      instruction when it reports no data (x86 CF=0, aarch64 NZCV.Z=1,
      riscv64 BIST/WAIT) before it gives the source up for this boot, with
      the ISA's pause hint between tries. RDSEED and RNDRRS wait for a
      fresh conditioner output and can fail many times in a row under
      load; Intel's 10-retry guidance is for RDRAND only. Bounded either
      way: a dead source costs this many tries once, never a hang.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TPM 2.0 (crates/drivers/tpm, kernel/src/boot/tpm.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One native driver, three users: an entropy source for the pool, the boot
# measurements (PCR extends) and, when chosen below, the OTA anti-rollback
# floor. The TPM is found, never assumed: x86_64 reads the ACPI TPM2 table
# (TIS over MMIO), riscv64 and aarch64 read the device tree for a
# `tcg,tpm_tis-spi` node and talk to it through the `Spi` device interface.
# No TPM, or a TPM that does not answer, leaves the boot exactly as it was.

config TPM
    # cargo-feature: tpm
    bool "TPM 2.0 (entropy, measured boot, OTA rollback floor)"
    default y if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      Kernel feature `tpm`. At the entropy step of the boot the kernel
      looks for a TPM 2.0 (x86_64: the ACPI TPM2 table with the TIS/FIFO
      start method; riscv64, aarch64: a device-tree node compatible with
      `tcg,tpm_tis-spi`, reached through the `Spi` interface on bus
      TPM_SPI_BUS). It sends Startup (an already-started TPM is fine),
      draws random bytes into the entropy pool (ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE), and
      extends each boot stage's digest into a PCR (TPM_PCR_BASE) while
      writing one decision record per extend. A CRB-only TPM (ACPI start
      methods 7 and 8) is reported and left alone. Polling driver: the
      boot waits on the TPM for at most the PTP timeouts (a few seconds)
      and only on a board that has one.

      Cost: no TPM present, a table lookup and a window probe at boot;
      with a TPM, a few commands at boot (milliseconds each). n: none of
      it is compiled.

choice
    prompt "TPM random bytes as a pool seed source"
    default ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_CREDIT
    depends on TPM
    help
      The TPM's GetRandom (48 bytes, two commands) is mixed into the
      kernel entropy pool (kernel/src/boot/entropy.rs `install_entropy`),
      after virtio-rng and the CPU instruction and independently of them.

      credit: mixed and credited, so a TPM alone seeds the pool on a board
      with no other source (Linux's hwrng gives the TPM a quality of
      1000). mix-only: mixed, never credited. off: never asked.

    config ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_CREDIT
        bool "credit: mix and credit (trust the TPM)"
    config ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_MIX
        bool "mix-only: mix, never credit"
    config ENTROPY_TPM_SOURCE_OFF
        bool "off: never ask the TPM for random bytes"
endchoice

config TPM_PCR_BASE
    int "First PCR of the boot measurements"
    depends on TPM
    range 0 21
    default 8
    help
      The boot stages are extended into three consecutive SHA-256 PCRs,
      in the order the boot learns them: TPM_PCR_BASE the kernel image
      (its build-id: SHA-256 of the linked text, LIVEPATCH on, else of the
      text as it stands once the TPM is found), +1 the signed
      configuration (CONFIG.INI as accepted), +2 the topology (CAPS.TOM as
      accepted, or the built-in topology's fixed name). PCRs 0-7 belong to
      firmware; 8 is where the OS-defined range starts, and a verifier
      that quotes PCRs 8-10 sees all three stages.

config TPM_SPI_BUS
    int "SPI bus the device-tree TPM sits on"
    depends on TPM && !ARCH_X86_64
    range 0 255
    default 0
    help
      The `Spi` interface names a device by bus and chip select. The tree
      gives the chip select (the node's `reg`) and the clock
      (`spi-max-frequency`) but not the driver's bus number, which is
      the SPI driver's own numbering; every board has one controller
      today, bus 0.
